From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ruiyangke <me@ry.ke>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 00:00:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] gpui_ios: Hardware keyboard, paste and terminal keys

A hardware keyboard on iPad reached GPUI only as text, so arrows, Escape,
Tab, function keys and shortcuts did nothing, and a software keyboard had no
way to send the keys a terminal needs.

Hardware keys that aren't text go to GPUI as key events, named from the HID
usage so they don't depend on the keyboard layout; text still goes through
UIKit's text system, which input methods compose with. The view takes hardware
keys while no text field has the keyboard. A text field that takes key
presses (`TextInputConfiguration::terminal_keys`) gets Escape, a latched
Control, Tab and the arrows on the software keyboard's shortcuts bar. Text
assistance off also turns off smart quotes and dashes.

The software keyboard comes up for focus the person asked for (a recent touch,
or a hardware keyboard attached), not for focus an app moves on its own, such
as a window opening with a field focused. A tap that put the caret in a field
that already had focus brings it up too; remote windows need that because they
keep one GPUI focus for all their fields.

A paste the person starts (Command-V, a menu) comes as UIKit's `paste:`.
Reading the pasteboard while UIKit runs it needs no permission; any other
read asks ("Paste from Other Apps"). `paste:` saves the pasteboard's text,
posts `PASTE_NOTIFICATION` so other code can save what it needs, and presses
Command-V in GPUI. Later reads of the same copy use the saved text for 30
seconds, as the copy may be a password. Paste is offered only while the
pasteboard holds something; counting items reads no contents, so it never
asks.

Remove when upstream gpui_ios handles hardware keys, paste and terminal
input on the keyboard.
